 ### Ray Grouping
-An optimization that improves performance by lowering the total number of marching steps by decreasing the total number of initial rays, grouping rays together into blocks [S26, unofficial]. Groups rays together during rendering to reduce total execution time [S26, unofficial]. Split groups of rays into smaller groups, though the implementation was noted to be slightly too eager to split groups [S26, unofficial]. Improvements were more significant for higher resolutions, as the cost at each step for a given group is not dependent on the number of rays in the group [S26, unofficial]. Tested on 64x64 and 128x128 pixel displays with 3 spheres at varying distances. Group size 2 lowered execution time. Sizes 4 and 8 did not decrease significantly further. Relative performance improved slightly more on the 128x128 display. Table 5.2 shows clock cycles: 64x64 display drops from 128k (size 1) to 84k (size 2), then 80k/82k. 128x128 drops from 512k to 328k, then 312k/313k [S26, unofficial]. Ray grouping reduces rays with similar execution flows, making it less effective on traditional lockstepped architectures compared to the custom GPU, though still potentially useful [S26, unofficial]. Implemented in the GLSL shader. Group size 1 is equivalent to no grouping [S26, unofficial].
