 ### Bounding Sphere Optimization
-An optimization that decreases the number of calculations made in each march by enclosing several objects into one bounding sphere [S26, unofficial]. Groups multiple objects within a single bounding sphere to optimize ray-object intersection tests during sphere tracing [S26, unofficial]. Objects should be bound in an adaptive way, enclosing objects depending on how they are grouped per frame, approximating the sphere definition for increased performance rather than computing it exactly [S26, unofficial]. Performance gain increases as the number of objects inside the sphere increases [S26, unofficial]. Performance gain is situational, depending on enclosed object count, spacing, and fit. In the tested scene, a gain occurs when >3 objects are enclosed. At 10 enclosed objects, FPS increases by 25%. Performance decreases if <4 objects are enclosed [S26, unofficial]. Bounding Spheres can work together with Orthogonal Culling by culling bounding spheres instead of single objects, decreasing the number of orthogonal projections made [S26, unofficial]. Implemented and tested in the GLSL shader. Requires measuring FPS against an identical unoptimized scene [S26, unofficial].
